WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.500
These mechanisms were designed by AI agents,

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.567
tested in physics simulation,

00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:07.467
then printed, and assembled.

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:17.233
AI agents can now write CAD assemblies that look convincing.

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:21.967
But a machine has to work. It must move, transmit force, and do it again.

00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:27.467
Ask an agent for a cargo bicycle, and its steering is blocked by its own frame.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:36.433
Give it a simulator, and it tests what is easy to test: the clock's escapement for ten simulated minutes, the striker on its own.

00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:41.867
The full alarm sequence is never checked, and the hour wheel collides with the alarm lever.

00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.933
FunCAD starts from the request itself.

00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:52.000
Reach the rear cup, without moving the cups in front.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.933
It breaks the request into functional requirements, and turns each one into a physics test.

00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:05.700
The harness binds every test to this candidate's own parts and joints, so the agent no longer decides what gets checked.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:14.600
It runs rigid-body simulation and geometry audits, and when a test fails, it reports which requirement failed, and by how much.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.500
Every candidate runs its own tests.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.067
This gripper passed the agent's own short test.

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:29.933
The harness tests repeated use. After the first release, the part tips over, and the second grasp measures zero newtons.

00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:36.800
With that diagnosis, the agent widens the pads and adds a rest, and the second grasp holds.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.700
We built FunCADBench: forty-six mechanism families,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.233
from clamps and transmissions to counters, alarm clocks, and coin sorters, across five kinds of mechanical demand.

00:01:54.733 --> 00:02:07.267
Each task fixes what a machine must do, not how it is built. On the slider-crank task, four agents built four different mechanisms, from eight parts to twenty-four, and all four meet the same requirements.

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:11.767
Giving agents a simulator helps.

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:19.167
Harness-directed tests help far more. Astra rises from eight to twenty-two tasks, and Grok from ten to nineteen.

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:23.933
Reduce the feedback to pass or fail, and most of that gain disappears.

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:38.733
And strong visual 3D skill does not make the best mechanical designer. Fable leads single attempts, and Grok, keeping its history, reaches thirty-six of forty-six, at a little over half of Astra's cost.

00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:41.667
The most complex mechanisms remain open.

00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:48.100
All of these tests run in simulation.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:56.067
To bring the designs into the real world, we printed and assembled six of them, including the cup machine from that first request.

00:02:56.867 --> 00:02:58.333
So, can it actually work?

00:02:58.733 --> 00:02:59.967
Now, we can test it.
